  <section id="attainable" class="py-section bg-cream-50">
    <div class="container-wide">
      <div class="max-w-3xl">
        <p class="eyebrow">The Honest Part</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 font-display text-3xl text-forest-700 md:text-4xl">"This all looks expensive."</h2>
        <div class="prose-body mt-5">
          <p>We hear this often, and it is a fair reaction. Renderings like these usually introduce homes most people cannot afford, and plenty of projects have promised otherwise and not delivered. We share the concern, and we would rather name it than decorate around it.</p>
          <p>So here is the honest version: attainability is not something we sprinkle on at the end. It is the hardest problem in the project, and it is the one we have organized the company around. Four things have to work together, and none of them is a slogan.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
        <div class="rounded-md border border-stone-100 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow !text-[10px]">Lever one</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-forest-700">Design that spends where it shows</h3>
          <p class="prose-body mt-3 !text-[15px]">Efficient plans, simple structure, and repeated details, so the budget goes into daylight, air, quiet, and durability rather than square footage you pay for and rarely use. Smaller homes only work when they are genuinely well designed, so that is where the design effort concentrates.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-stone-100 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow !text-[10px]">Lever two</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-forest-700">Building the same thing more than once</h3>
          <p class="prose-body mt-3 !text-[15px]">Most homes are built as one-offs, and the industry pays for that in time, waste, and rework. Repeating a small number of well-tested home types, with as much built under a roof as makes sense, is how quality goes up while cost comes down. It works when there is committed demand, which is exactly why we are asking you now instead of later.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-stone-100 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow !text-[10px]">Lever three</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-forest-700">The neighborhood carries some of the home</h3>
          <p class="prose-body mt-3 !text-[15px]">A courtyard, a market, a workshop, a guest room you can book: shared things you would otherwise pay for privately, or go without. Sharing them well is what lets a modest home live much larger, and it is why we design the block and the home together.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="rounded-md border border-stone-100 bg-white p-6">
          <p class="eyebrow !text-[10px]">Lever four</p>
          <h3 class="mt-1 font-display text-xl text-forest-700">Patient money, and lower bills</h3>
          <p class="prose-body mt-3 !text-[15px]">Capital that expects returns over decades rather than a quick exit changes what a home can cost. Add energy bills engineered down and a life that needs fewer car trips, and the monthly math looks different from the sticker alone. We are building the ownership structures for this in the open, and we will publish what we learn.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-8 max-w-3xl rounded-md border border-copper-400/40 bg-white p-6">
        <p class="prose-body !text-[15px]">What we will not do is give you a number we cannot stand behind yet. Land, financing, and construction costs are still moving, and any price quoted today would be marketing rather than information. What we can promise: a real range as soon as we have one we believe, published to this list first, along with the reasoning behind it. If we cannot make attainable work here, we would rather tell you that than quietly build something else.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

